== Chapter VIII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[Indwelling sin proved powerful from its deceit. Proved to be deceitful. The general nature of deceit. Jas 1:14 explained. How the mind is drawn away from its duty by the deceitfulness of sin. The principal duties of the mind in our obedience. The ways and means by which it is turned from them.]
]

#smallcaps[The] second part of the evidence of the #emph[power of sin] from its manner of working is taken from its #emph[deceitfulness.] In its working it adds #emph[deceit] to #emph[power.] Where power and deceit are combined, the efficacy of that combination must be great, especially when it is helped and assisted by all the ways and means already dwelt on. All who value their souls must carefully watch against it.

Before we come to show what the nature of this #emph[deceitfulness of sin] consists in, and how it prevails by it, some testimonies will briefly be given to the #emph[thing itself,] and some light given on its #emph[general nature.]

That sin, #emph[indwelling sin,] is deceitful, we have the express testimony of the #emph[Holy Spirit] (Heb 3:13): “so that none of you#super[+] may be hardened by sin’s deceitfulness.” It is deceitful. Take heed of it, watch against it, or it will produce its utmost effect in #emph[hardening the heart] against God. It is on account of sin that the #emph[heart] is said to be “deceitful above all things” (Jer 17:9). Take a man in other things, and, as #emph[Job] says, though he “would be wise and crafty, he is like the colt of a wild donkey” (Job 11:12): a poor, vain, empty nothing. But consider his #emph[heart] on account of this law of sin, and it is #emph[crafty] and deceitful above all things. “They are skilled in doing evil,” says the prophet, but they do not know how to do good (Jer 4:22). The apostle speaks to the same purpose in Eph 4:22: “the old man, which is being corrupted by its deceitful lusts.” Every lust, which is a branch of this law of sin, is #emph[deceitful.] And where there is poison in every stream, the fountain must be corrupt. No particular lust has any #emph[deceit] in it except what is passed on to it from this fountain of all actual lust, this #emph[law of sin.] And the coming of the man of sin is said to be in and with “the deception of unrighteousness” (2 Thess 2:10). Unrighteousness is generally condemned and spoken against among men, so it is not easy to see how any man could make use of it to gain a reputation. But there is a #emph[deceptiveness] in it, by which the minds of men are turned aside from a proper consideration of it, as we will show later. And so the account the apostle gives of those who are under the power of sin is that they are “deceived” (Titus 3:3). And the life of evil men is nothing but “deceiving and being deceived” (2 Tim 3:13). So we have sufficient testimony given to this quality of the #emph[enemy] we have to deal with: he is #emph[deceitful.] Of all things, this consideration puts the mind of man at a loss in dealing with an adversary. He knows he can have no security against one who is #emph[deceitful] except by standing on his own guard and defense all his days.

To show further the strength and advantage that sin has by its #emph[deceit,] we may observe that the Scripture for the most part places deceit as the head and spring of every sin, as though no sin #emph[followed] except where #emph[deceit] went before. So it is in 1 Tim 2:13, 14. The reason the apostle gives why #emph[Adam,] though he was #emph[formed first,] was not #emph[first in the transgression,] is that he was not #emph[first deceived.] The woman, though made last, was #emph[deceived first,] and so she was first in the sin. Even that first sin began in deceit, and until the mind was #emph[deceived,] the soul was safe. #emph[Eve,] therefore, truly expressed the matter in Gen 3:13, though she did not do it to a good end. “The serpent deceived me,” she says, “and I ate.” She thought to lessen her own crime by laying the charge on the #emph[serpent.] And this was a new fruit of the sin she had thrown herself into. But the fact of the matter was true: she was #emph[deceived] before she ate, and deceit went before the transgression. And the apostle shows that sin and Satan still take the same course (2 Cor 11:3). There is, he says, the same way of working toward actual sin as there was of old. Deceiving and beguiling go before, and sin, which is its actual accomplishment, follows after. That is why the devil does all the great works he does in the world, to stir men up to oppose the Lord Jesus Christ and his kingdom, by #emph[deceit] (Rev 12:9): “the devil … the deceiver of the whole world.” It would be utterly impossible for men to be persuaded to remain in his service, carrying out his designs to their eternal ruin, and sometimes their temporal ruin too, if they were not exceedingly #emph[deceived.] See also Rev 20:10.

That is why we are given so many warnings to take heed that #emph[we are not deceived,] if we would take heed that we do not sin. See Eph 5:6; 1 Cor 6:9; 1 Cor 15:33; Gal 6:7; Luke 21:8. From all these testimonies we may learn the influence that #emph[deceit] has on #emph[sin,] and so the advantage the #emph[law of sin] has for putting forth its power by its #emph[deceitfulness.] Where it succeeds in deceiving, it does not fail to bring forth its fruit.

The ground of this efficacy of sin by deceit is taken from the #emph[faculty of the soul] that is affected by it. Deceit properly affects the #emph[mind]\; it is the #emph[mind] that is deceived. When sin attempts any other way of entrance into the soul, as by the #emph[affections,] the mind keeps its right and sovereignty and is able to check and control it. But where the mind is tainted, sin’s prevailing power must be great. For the #emph[mind,] or understanding, is the leading faculty of the soul. What the mind fixes on, the will and affections rush after, since they are capable of no consideration except what the mind presents to them. That is why, though the entanglement of the #emph[affections] in sin is often most troublesome, the deceit of the #emph[mind] is always most dangerous. This is because of the place the mind holds in the soul with regard to all its workings. Its office is to #emph[guide, direct,] choose, and lead. And if the light that is in us is darkness, how great is that darkness!

This will appear further if we consider the nature of #emph[deceit] in general. It consists in presenting things to the soul, or mind, #emph[otherwise than they are,] either in their nature, causes, effects, or present relation to the soul. This is the general nature of #emph[deceit,] and it prevails in many ways. It #emph[hides] what ought to be seen and considered. It conceals circumstances and consequences. It presents what is not, or presents things #emph[as they are not,] as we will show later in particular. It was shown before that Satan beguiled and #emph[deceived] our first parents; that is the term the Holy Spirit gives to his temptation and seduction. And the Scripture relates how he deceived them (Gen 3:4, 5). He did it by representing things #emph[otherwise] than they were. The fruit was #emph[desirable]\; that was plain to the eye. Satan takes advantage of this to suggest secretly that what God aimed at in forbidding them to eat of it was merely to #emph[cut short] their happiness. He #emph[hides] from them that it was a trial of their obedience, and that certain, though not immediate, ruin would follow upon their eating it. He puts forward only the present advantage of knowledge, and so presents the whole case to them quite otherwise than it really was. This is the nature of #emph[deceit.] It is a representation of a matter under disguises. It hides what is undesirable and puts forward what is really not in the matter, so that the mind may make a false judgment of it. In this way #emph[Jacob] deceived #emph[Isaac] by his brother’s clothing and the skins on his hands and neck.

Again, #emph[deceit] has an advantage from the way of managing things that is inseparable from it. It is always carried on by #emph[degrees,] little by little, so that the whole of the design and aim in hand is not discovered all at once. That is how #emph[Satan] dealt in that great #emph[deceit] just mentioned. He goes on in it by steps and degrees. First he removes an objection and tells them #emph[they will not die.] Then he sets before them #emph[the good of knowledge] and their being #emph[like God] by it. To hide and conceal ends, to go on by #emph[steps] and degrees, to make use of what has been gained and from there to press on to further effects: this is the true nature of deceit. #emph[Stephen] tells us that the #emph[king of Egypt dealt craftily,] or #emph[deceitfully,] with their #emph[kindred] (Acts 7:19). We may see how he did it in Exod 1. He did not at first fall to #emph[killing] and #emph[slaying] them, but says (v. 10), “Come, let us #emph[deal shrewdly] with them,” and begins to oppress them. This brings about their #emph[bondage] (v. 11). Having gained this ground and made them slaves, he goes on to #emph[destroy their children] (v. 16). He did not fall on them all at once, but by degrees. This may be enough to show in general that sin is #emph[deceitful,] and the advantages it has by it.

For the way, manner, and progress of sin in working by #emph[deceit,] we have it fully expressed in Jas 1:14, 15: “Each one is tempted when he is drawn away and enticed by his own lust. Then, when lust has conceived, it gives birth to sin; and sin, when it is finished, gives birth to death.” Since this place declares the whole of what we aim at in this matter, we must dwell on it in particular.

In the #emph[previous verse] the apostle shows that men are willing to carry on the #emph[old trade] that our first parents #emph[set up] at the entrance of sin, that is, excusing themselves in their sins and casting the occasion and blame of them on others. It is not, they say, from themselves, from their own nature and inclinations or their own designs, that they have committed such and such evils, but merely from their temptations. And if they do not know where to lay the evil of those temptations, they will lay it on God himself rather than go without an excuse or something to lessen their guilt. The apostle rebukes this evil in the hearts of men in #emph[verse] 13: “When tempted, no one should say, ‘God is tempting me.’ For God cannot be tempted by evil, nor does He tempt anyone.” And to show how just this rebuke is, in the words mentioned he uncovers the true causes of the rise and whole progress of sin. He shows that the whole #emph[guilt] of it lies on the #emph[sinner,] and that the whole #emph[punishment] of it will also be his lot, unless it is graciously prevented.

So, as was said, in these words we have the whole progress of lust, or indwelling sin, by way of subtlety, fraud, and deceit, expressed and marked out by the Holy Spirit. From them we will show the particular ways and means by which it puts forth its power and efficacy in the hearts of men by #emph[deceitfulness] and subtlety. And we may observe in the words:

First, the utmost end aimed at in all the actings of sin, or what it tends to in its own nature. That is #emph[death]: “sin, when it is finished, gives birth to death,” the everlasting death of the sinner. Whatever it pretends, this is the #emph[end] it aims at and tends to. Hiding ends and designs is the principal property of deceit. Sin does this to the utmost. It pleads countless other things, but it never once declares that it aims at the death, the everlasting death, of the soul. And a fixed apprehension of this #emph[end] of every sin is a blessed means of preventing its prevailing power in its way of deceiving or beguiling.

Secondly, the general way it acts toward that end is by #emph[temptation]\; “each one is tempted by his own lust.” I do not intend to speak in general of the nature of #emph[temptations.] That does not belong to our present purpose, and besides, I have done it elsewhere. For now it is enough to observe that the life of temptation lies in deceit. So in the business of sin, to be effectually #emph[tempted] and to be #emph[beguiled] or #emph[deceived] are the same thing. So it was in the first temptation. As was shown before, it is everywhere called the serpent’s beguiling or deceiving: “Eve was deceived by the serpent’s cunning,” that is, he prevailed over her by his temptations. So every man is #emph[tempted,] that is, every man is #emph[beguiled] or deceived, by his own lust, or #emph[indwelling sin,] which we have often declared to be the same thing.

=== The Degrees of Sin’s Deceit

The degrees by which sin goes on in this work of #emph[tempting] or #emph[deceiving] are #emph[five.] For we showed before that it belongs to the nature of deceit to work by #emph[degrees,] using its advantage at one step to gain another.

The first of these consists in drawing off, or drawing away: “each one is tempted when he is drawn away by his own lust.”

The second is in enticing: “and enticed.”

The third is in the conception of sin: “when lust has conceived.” When the heart is enticed, then lust conceives in it.

The fourth is the bringing forth of sin in its actual accomplishment: “when lust has conceived, it gives birth to sin.” In all this there is a hidden allusion to an adulterous straying from marriage duties, and to conceiving and bringing forth children of whoredom and fornication.

The fifth is the #emph[finishing of sin,] its completing, the filling up of its measure, by which the end lust first designed is brought about: “sin, when it is finished, gives birth to death.” As lust conceiving naturally and necessarily brings forth sin, so sin finished infallibly brings about eternal death.

The #emph[first] of these relates to the #emph[mind,] which is drawn off, or drawn away, by the deceit of sin. The #emph[second] relates to the #emph[affections,] which are enticed or entangled. The #emph[third] relates to the #emph[will,] in which sin is conceived; the consent of the will is the formal conception of actual sin. The #emph[fourth] relates to the #emph[conduct,] in which sin is #emph[brought forth]\; it shows itself in the lives and courses of men. The #emph[fifth] concerns a #emph[hardened course] in sinning, which finishes, completes, and closes up the whole work of sin, and on which #emph[death,] or eternal ruin, follows.

I will chiefly consider the #emph[first three,] in which the main strength of the deceit of sin lies. I do so because in #emph[believers,] whose state and condition is chiefly set forth for consideration here, God is pleased for the most part graciously to prevent the #emph[fourth instance,] that is, #emph[the bringing forth of actual sins] in their conduct. And he always and wholly prevents the last, that is, their becoming hardened in a course of sin until it is finished. What ways God in his grace and faithfulness uses to #emph[stifle the conceptions of sin] in the womb, and to hinder its actual production in the lives of men, must be spoken of later. We will dwell fully, then, on the #emph[first three instances,] as those in which believers are chiefly concerned in this matter.

=== Drawing the Mind Away from Its Duty

The first thing sin is said to do, working by way of deceit, is to draw away, or draw off. So a man is said to be #emph[drawn off,] or #emph[drawn away] and diverted, that is, from #emph[attending] to that course of obedience and holiness which, in opposition to sin and its law, he is bound to attend to with diligence.

Now it is on the #emph[mind] that this effect of the #emph[deceit] of sin is worked. The mind, or understanding, as we have shown, is the guiding, leading faculty of the soul. It goes before in discerning, judging, and determining, to make the way of moral actions plain and smooth for the will and affections. It is to the soul what #emph[Moses] told his father-in-law he might be to the people in the wilderness, #emph[eyes to guide them,] keeping them from wandering in that desolate place. It is the #emph[eye] of the soul. Without its guidance the will and affections would wander perpetually in the wilderness of this world, after any #emph[object] that offered or presented itself to them with the appearance of a #emph[present good.]

So the first thing sin aims at in its #emph[deceitful working] is to draw off and divert the mind from carrying out its duty.

Two things belong to the duty of the mind in the #emph[special office] it has in and concerning the obedience God requires.

First, to keep itself and the whole soul in such a frame and posture as will make it ready for all duties of obedience, and watchful against all enticements to the conception of sin.

Secondly, in particular, to attend carefully to all particular actions, so that they are performed #emph[as] God requires, in matter, manner, time, and season, agreeable to his will; and also to head off all particular #emph[offers] of sin in things forbidden. The whole duty of the mind of a believer consists in these two things. And #emph[indwelling sin] tries to divert it and draw it off from both of them.

The first of these is the duty of the mind with regard to the general frame and course of the whole soul. Two things may be considered here. First, that it is founded on a proper and constant consideration: 1. of ourselves, of sin and its vileness; 2. of God, of his grace and goodness. And sin labors to draw the mind off from both of these. Secondly, that it consists in attending to those #emph[duties] that are suited in a special way to head off the working of the law of sin.

First, sin tries to draw the mind off from a proper consideration, apprehension, and sense of sin’s own #emph[vileness] and of the danger that goes with it. We will give this as our first instance. A proper and constant consideration of sin ought always to stay with us: of sin in its nature, in all its aggravating circumstances, in its end and tendency, especially as it is represented in the blood and cross of Christ. Jer 2:19: “Consider and realize how evil and bitter it is for you to forsake the #smallcaps[Lord] your God.” Every sin is a #emph[forsaking] of the Lord our God. If the heart does not know, if it does not consider, that sin is an evil and bitter thing, #emph[evil] in itself and #emph[bitter] in its effects, fruit, and outcome, it will never be secured against it. Besides, the frame of heart most accepted by God in any sinner is the #emph[humble,] contrite, self-abasing frame. Isa 57:15: “For thus says the One who is high and lifted up, who inhabits eternity, whose name is Holy: ‘I dwell in a high and holy place, and with the contrite and humble in spirit, to restore the spirit of the lowly and revive the heart of the contrite.’” See also Luke 18:13, 14. This is fitting for a sinner; no garment sits so becomingly on him. “Clothe yourselves with humility,” says the apostle (1 Pet 5:5). It is what befits us, and it is the only safe frame. He who walks humbly walks safely. This is the design of #emph[Peter’s] advice in 1 Pet 1:17: “conduct yourselves in reverent fear during your stay as foreigners.” After he himself had fallen through another frame of mind, he gives this advice to all believers. It is not a bondage, a servile fear, that disquiets and perplexes the soul. It is such a fear as may keep men constantly #emph[calling on the Father] with regard to the final judgment, so that they may be preserved from sin, which they were in such great danger of. That is what he urges on them: “Since you#super[+] call on a Father who judges each one’s work impartially, conduct yourselves in reverent fear during your stay as foreigners.” This is the humble frame of soul. And how is it obtained? How is it preserved? In no other way than by a constant, deep apprehension of the evil, vileness, and danger of sin. That is how it was produced and kept up in the approved tax collector. “God,” he says, “have mercy on me, a sinner!” A sense of sin kept him humble, and humility opened his way to a testimony of the pardon of sin.

And by grace this is the great #emph[preservative] from sin, as we have an example in #emph[Joseph] (Gen 39:9). Under the pressure of his great temptation, he falls back at once into this frame of spirit. “How,” he says, “could I do this great evil and sin against God?” A constant, steady sense of the evil of sin gives him such preservation that he risks liberty and life in opposing it. To fear sin is to fear the Lord. So the holy man tells us that they are the same (Job 28:28): “The fear of the Lord, that is wisdom, and to turn away from evil is understanding.”

So this, first of all and in general, is what the #emph[law of sin] puts forth its deceit about: to draw the #emph[mind] away from this frame, which is the strongest fort of the soul’s defense and security. It labors to divert the mind from a proper apprehension of the vileness, abomination, and danger of sin. It secretly and imperceptibly slips in #emph[lessening,] excusing, extenuating thoughts of it. Or it draws the mind off from pondering it, from dwelling on it in its thoughts as much as it ought and as much as it once did. Suppose the heart of a man has been made tender, soft, and deeply aware of sin through the word, Spirit, and grace of Christ. If afterward, on any account or by any means whatever, it comes to have less, fewer, slighter, or less moving thoughts of sin or about it, then the mind of that man is #emph[drawn away] by the #emph[deceitfulness of sin.]

==== The Abuse of Gospel Grace

There are two ways among others by which the law of sin tries #emph[deceitfully] to draw the mind off from this duty, and from the frame that follows from it.

First, it does it by a horrible #emph[abuse of gospel grace.] In the #emph[gospel] a remedy is provided against the whole evil of sin: its filth, its guilt, and all its dangerous consequences. It is the doctrine of the deliverance of the souls of men from sin and death, a revelation of the gracious will of God toward sinners by Jesus Christ. Now what is the true tendency of this doctrine, of this revelation of grace? And what ought we to use it and improve it for? The apostle declares this in Titus 2:11, 12: “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to everyone. It instructs us to renounce ungodliness and worldly lusts, and to live sensible, upright, and godly lives in the present age.” This is what it teaches; this is what we ought to learn from it and by it. That is why #emph[universal holiness] is called “a manner of life worthy of the gospel” (Phil 1:27). It befits the gospel as what answers to its end, aim, and design, as what the gospel requires and what it ought to be improved to. And accordingly it produces this effect where its word is received and preserved in a saving light (Rom 12:2; Eph 4:20, 21, 22, 23, 24). But here the #emph[deceit] of sin puts itself in between. It separates the #emph[doctrine] of grace from its use and end. It stops at its notions and intercepts its influence in its proper application. From the doctrine of the #emph[assured pardon] of sin, it slips in a #emph[disregard] of sin. God in Christ lays down the #emph[premise,] and Satan and sin draw the #emph[conclusion.] For the apostle shows that the #emph[deceitfulness] of #emph[sin] is apt to plead for disregarding sin on the ground of the grace of God by which it is pardoned. He shows it in his rebuke and abhorrence of such a suggestion (Rom 6:1): “What then shall we say? Shall we continue in sin so that grace may increase? Certainly not!” Men’s deceitful hearts, he says, are apt to draw that conclusion, but far be it from us to give it any welcome. Yet #emph[Jude] declares (v. 4) that some have plainly improved that deceit to their own eternal ruin: “ungodly ones … who turn the grace of our God into a license for immorality.” And we have had dreadful instances of it in the days of temptation in which we have lived.

Indeed, much of the #emph[wisdom] of faith and the power of gospel grace lies in opposing this deceit. The mind may be fully possessed with the notion and doctrine of gospel truth about the full and free forgiveness of all sins in the blood of Christ, and cast habitually and firmly into its mold. To be able then to keep the heart always in a deep, humbling sense of sin, in abhorrence of it and self-abasement for it, is a great effect of gospel wisdom and grace. This is the trial and touchstone of gospel #emph[light.] If it keeps the heart aware of sin, humble, lowly, and broken on that account; if it teaches us to water a free pardon with tears, to detest forgiven sin, to watch diligently for the ruin of that which we are nevertheless assured will never ruin us, then it is #emph[divine,] from above, from the Spirit of grace. If it secretly and imperceptibly makes men loose and #emph[slight] in their thoughts about sin, it is adulterated, selfish, and false. If it will be everything and answer every end, it is nothing.

So it comes about that at times we see men walking in a #emph[bondage frame of spirit] all their days, low in their light and poor in their apprehensions of grace, so that it is hard to tell to which covenant they belong in their principles, whether they are under the law or under grace. Yet they walk with a more conscientious tenderness about sinning than many who have advanced to higher degrees of light and knowledge than they. It is not that the saving light of the gospel is not the #emph[only principle] of saving holiness and obedience. It is that through the deceitfulness of sin this light is abused in various ways, to favor the soul in many neglects of duties, and to draw the #emph[mind] off from a proper consideration of the nature, desert, and danger of sin. And this is done in several ways.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, the soul often needs relief by gospel grace against a sense of the guilt of sin and the accusation of the law. So at length it comes to make this a #emph[common and ordinary thing,] something that may be done slightly. It has found a good medicine for its wounds, one whose efficacy it has experienced, and it comes to apply it #emph[slightly,] so that it skins over its sores rather than cures them. A little less earnestness, a little less diligence serves each time, until perhaps the soul begins to assure itself of #emph[pardon as a matter of course.] And this tends directly to #emph[draw the mind off] from its constant and universal watchfulness against sin. Suppose a man’s light has made his way of access plain for obtaining pardon. Unless he is very watchful, he is far more apt to become superficial, formal, and careless in his work than the man who, because of #emph[mists] and #emph[darkness,] has to search about to find his way rightly to the throne of grace. A man who has often traveled a road passes on without paying attention or asking the way. But a man who is a stranger to it notices every turning and asks every passerby, and so makes his journey safer than the other does.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, the #emph[deceitfulness of sin] takes advantage of the #emph[doctrine of grace,] by many ways and means, to extend the bounds of the soul’s liberty beyond what God has assigned to it. Some have never thought themselves free from a legal bondage frame until they have been brought to the #emph[borders] of sensuality, and some into its #emph[depths.] How often sin will plead: “This strictness, this exactness, this anxious care is in no way necessary. Relief is provided in the gospel against such things. Would you live as though there were no need of the gospel? As though the pardon of sin were for nothing?” But we will have occasion to speak more of these pleas of sin from gospel grace later, in particular.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, in times of #emph[temptation] this deceitfulness of sin will argue expressly #emph[for sin] from gospel grace. At least it will plead for these two things.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, that there is no need for such a tenacious and severe contending against sin as the principle of the #emph[new creature] is set on. If it cannot divert the soul or mind #emph[wholly] from attending to temptations so as to oppose them, it will still try to draw believers off with regard to the #emph[manner] of their attending. They need not use the diligence that the soul at first thinks necessary.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it will keep offering relief about the outcome of sin: that it will not turn to the ruin or destruction of the soul, because it is, or will be, or may be pardoned by the grace of the gospel. And this is true. This is the great and only relief of the soul against sin whose guilt it has already contracted, the blessed and only remedy for a guilty soul. But when it is pleaded and called to mind by the deceitfulness of sin, in compliance with a temptation to sin, then it is #emph[poison.] Poison is mixed into every drop of this balm, to the danger, if not the death, of the soul. This is the first way by which the #emph[deceitfulness of sin] draws the mind off from properly attending to that sense of sin’s vileness which alone is able to keep the mind in the humble, self-abased frame that is acceptable to God. It makes the #emph[mind] careless, as though its work were needless because grace abounds. This is like a soldier who neglects his post because he trusts in a reserve, when that reserve is provided only on the condition that he keeps his own proper place.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, in this matter of #emph[drawing the mind off] from a proper sense of sin, sin takes advantage to work by its deceit from the state and condition of men in the world. I will give only one instance of how it goes about this. In their younger days men naturally have affections that are quicker, more vigorous and active, and more sensibly at work in them than afterward. As to their sensible working and operation, the affections #emph[naturally] decay, and many things befall men in their lives that take off their edge and keenness. But as men lose in their affections, they grow and improve in their understandings, resolutions, and judgments, unless they are besotted with sensuality or with the corruption that is in the world through lust. So if what once had a place in their #emph[affections] does not take a place in their #emph[minds] and #emph[judgments,] they lose those things entirely; such things have no more place in their souls. So men have no regard for, indeed they utterly despise, the things their affections were set on with delight and greed in their childhood. But if those things have by some means come to be fixed in their minds and judgments, men keep a high esteem for them and cling to them as closely as they did when their affections were more vigorous. It is only as if those things have changed their seat in the soul. It is the same in spiritual things. The first and chief seat of the sense of sin is in the #emph[affections.] As the affections are great and large in natural youth, so they are in spiritual youth (Jer 2:2): “I remember the devotion of your youth, your love as a bride.” Besides, such persons have newly come out of their convictions, in which they have been cut to the heart and so made tender. Whatever touches a wound is felt thoroughly. So it is with the guilt of sin before the wound given by conviction is thoroughly healed. But now, when the affections begin to decay naturally, they begin to decay also in their sensible actings and motions in spiritual things. Though men improve in grace, they may decay in feeling. At least, spiritual feeling is not #emph[rooted] in the affections, but is only passed on to them. Now in these decays, unless the soul takes care to fix a deep sense of sin on the #emph[mind and judgment,] so that it may continually affect the heart and affections, that sense will decay. And here the deceit of the law of sin puts itself in. It lets the sense of sin decay in the affections, and it diverts the mind from holding a proper, constant, fixed consideration of sin. We may consider this a little in persons who never make any progress in the ways of God beyond conviction. How #emph[aware] of sin they will be for a season! How they will mourn and weep then under a sense of its guilt! How heartily and sincerely they will resolve against it! Their affections are vigorous and, as it were, rule in their souls. But they are like a plant that will flourish for a day or two with watering, though it has no root. For a while later we see that the more experience these men have had of sin, the less they fear it, as the wise man suggests (Eccl 8:11). At length they come to be the greatest despisers of sin in the world. There is no sinner like the man who has sinned away his convictions of sin. What is the reason for this? In their convictions the sense of sin was fixed on their affections. As it decayed there, they took no care to have it fixed deeply and graciously on their minds. The deceitfulness of sin robbed them of this, and so ruined their souls. In some measure it is the same with believers. As the feeling of the affections decays, as they grow heavy and dull, great decays will follow, unless great wisdom and grace are used to fix a proper sense of sin on the mind and judgment, which may provoke, excite, enliven, and stir up the affections every day. At first sorrow, trouble, grief, and fear affected the mind and would give it no rest. If afterward the mind does not affect the heart with sorrow and grief, the whole will be cast out, and the soul will be in danger of being hardened. These are some of the ways by which the #emph[deceit of sin] diverts the mind from the first part of its safe and preserving frame, or draws it off from its constant watchfulness against sin and all its effects.]

==== The Holy Consideration of God and His Grace

The second #emph[part] of this general duty of the mind is to keep the soul in a constant, holy consideration of God and his grace. This plainly lies at the spring of gospel obedience. The way by which sin draws the #emph[mind] off from this part of its duty is open and well enough known, though it is not watched against well enough. The Scripture everywhere declares it to be the filling of men’s minds #emph[with earthly things.] It sets this in direct opposition to the #emph[heavenly frame] of the mind, which is the spring of gospel obedience (Col 3:2): “Set your affections on things above, not on earthly things,” or set your minds. It is as if he had said, “You#super[+] cannot be set or fixed on both together, so as to mind them both principally and chiefly.” And the affections toward the one and the other, which come from these different principles of #emph[minding] the one or the other, are set against each other as directly inconsistent (1 John 2:15): “Do not love the world or anything in the world. If anyone loves the world, the love of the Father is not in him.” And actions in a course that suits these affections are also set forth as contrary: “You#super[+] cannot serve both God and money.” These are two masters whom no man can serve at the same time to the satisfaction of both. Every #emph[inordinate minding] of earthly things, then, is opposed to the frame in which our minds ought to be fixed on God and his grace in a course of gospel obedience.

There are several ways by which the #emph[deceitfulness of sin] draws the mind off in this particular. But the chief of them is by pressing these things on the mind as things that are #emph[lawful,] and perhaps necessary. So all those in the #emph[parable] who excused themselves from coming in to the wedding feast of the gospel did it on the ground that they were engaged in their #emph[lawful] callings: one with his #emph[farm,] another with his #emph[oxen,] the means by which he plowed in this world. By this plea the minds of men were drawn off from the frame of heavenliness required for our walking with God, and the rules of not loving the world, or of using it as though we did not use it, are neglected because of it. It is not my present business to declare what wisdom, what watchfulness, what serious and frequent trial and examination of ourselves is required to keep our hearts and minds in a heavenly frame in the use and pursuit of earthly things. This much is evident: the engine by which the deceit of sin #emph[draws off] and turns aside the mind in this matter is the pretense of the #emph[lawfulness] of the things it would have the mind occupy itself with. And very few are armed against this with sufficient diligence, wisdom, and skill. This is the first and most general attempt that #emph[indwelling sin] makes on the soul by #emph[deceit.] It draws the mind away from diligently attending to its course, in a proper sense of the evil of sin and a proper and constant consideration of God and his grace.
